      "id": 1,
      "text": "Prati takes its name from 14th‑century Roman tribune Cola di Rienzo, turning the street into a bustling hub where history meets commerce.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.archeoroma.org/sites/prati/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Prati became Rome’s twenty‑second rione on August 20, 1921, marking its official entry into the city’s municipal structure.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.archeoroma.org/sites/prati/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The headquarters of Italy’s national broadcaster RAI sit in Prati, making the district a key media hub for the country.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://italysegreta.com/a-locals-guide-to-prati/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Prati boasts three of Rome’s best shopping streets, alongside a smattering of great restaurants and bars that keep locals and visitors busy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://italysegreta.com/a-locals-guide-to-prati/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Lazio football fans trace their roots to Prati, where the club’s largest supporter section historically formed an arch across northern Rome.",
